W: 2. There came a troop of gentlemen
W: As they were riding by
W: And one of them has lighted down
W: And he's asked of her the way, way,
W: And he's asked of her the way.
W:
W: 3. "Ride on, ride on, you rank riders,
W: Your steeds are stout and strong,
W: For it's out of the fold I will not go
W: For fear you do me wrong, wrong,
W: For fear you do me wrong."
W:
W: 4. Now he's taken her by the middle jimp
W: And by the green gown sleeve,
W: And there he's had his will of her
W: And he's asked of her no leave, leave,
W: And he's asked of her no leave.
W:
W: 5. Now he's mounted on his berry brown steed,
W: He soon o'erta'en his men
W: And one and all cried out to him,
W: "Oh, master, you tarried long, long,
W: Oh, master, you tarried long."
W:
W: 6. "Oh, I've ridden East and I've ridden West,
W: And I've ridden o'er the downs,
W: But the bonniest lass that ever I saw
W: She was calling her sheep to the fold, fold,
W: She was calling her sheep to the fold."
W:
W: 7. She's taken the milk pail on her head
W: And she's gone lingering home.
W: And all her father said to her
W: Was, "Daughter, you tarried long, long,
W: Oh, daughter, you tarried long."
W:
W: 8. "Oh, woe betide your shepherd, father,
W: He takes no care of the sheep,
W: For he's builded the fold at the back of the down
W: And the fox has frightened me, me,
W: And the fox has frightened me."
W:
W: 9. "Oh, there came a fox to the fold door
W: With twinkling eye so bold,
W: And ere he'd taken the lamb that he did
W: I'd rather he'd taken them all, all,
W: I'd rather he'd taken them all."
W:
W: 10. Now twenty weeks were gone and past,
W: Twenty weeks and three,
W: The lassie began to fret and to frown
W: And to long for the twinkling eye, bright eye,
W: And to long for the twinkling eye.
W:
W: 11. Now it fell on a day, on a bonnie summer's day
W: That she walked out alone.
W: That self-same troop of gentlemen
W: Come a-riding over the down, down,
W: Come a-riding over the down.
W:
W: 12. "Who got the babe with thee, Bonny May,
W: Who got the babe in thy arms?"
W: For shame, she blushed, and aye, she said,
W: "Was I've a good man of my own, own,
W: Was I've a good man of my own."
W:
W: 13. "You lie, you lie, you Bonny, Bonny May,
W: So loud I hear you lie.
W: Remember the misty murky night
W: I lay in the fold with thee, thee,
W: I lay in the fold with thee.
W:
W: 14. Now he's mounted off his berry brown steed,
W: He's sat the fair May on.
W: "Go call out your kye, father, yourself,
W: She'll ne'er call them again, again,
W: She'll ne'er call them again."
W:
W: 15. Oh, he's Lord of twenty plough of land,
W: Twenty plough and three,
W: And he's taken away the bonniest lass
W: In all the South country, country,
W: In all the South country.
